# Replayable admission evidence

Evidence is an observation bound to one exact input digest. This notebook builds evidence,
proves its digest is a pure function of its content, breaks it on purpose, and replays an
admission to check that identical input yields identical digests.

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:ash, "~> 3.0"},
    {:ash_graphlaw, path: Path.expand("../..", __DIR__)}
  ],
  consolidate_protocols: false
)
```

## Evidence from an admitted transition

`AshGraphLaw.Admitted` is what the engine's `law` op answers on success. Build one from a
response-shaped map (the shape the wire format defines) and bind evidence to a projected input.

```elixir
input_text = "<urn:example:s> <urn:example:p> \"o\" .\n"
input_digest = :crypto.hash(:sha256, input_text) |> Base.encode16(case: :lower)

admitted =
  AshGraphLaw.Admitted.from_map(%{
    "states" => ["state-0"],
    "receipts" => [%{"step" => "shacl"}],
    "nquads" => ""
  })

evidence =
  AshGraphLaw.Evidence.new(:demo_admission, admitted, input_digest,
    wasm_sha256: String.duplicate("0", 64)
  )

%{
  admission: evidence.admission,
  standing: evidence.standing,
  input_digest_matches?: evidence.input_digest == input_digest,
  digest: evidence.digest
}
```

This evidence is constructed here, not produced by an engine run: its `wasm_sha256` is a
placeholder. It demonstrates the digest algebra, not an admission.

## The digest is recomputable

`digest/1` is the SHA-256 of a canonical, sorted-key JSON encoding that excludes the `digest`
field itself. Anyone holding the evidence can recompute it:

```elixir
recomputed = AshGraphLaw.Evidence.digest(evidence)
{recomputed == evidence.digest, byte_size(evidence.digest)}
```

Map ordering never changes it. Canonical JSON sorts keys recursively:

```elixir
a = AshGraphLaw.Evidence.canonical_json(%{b: 1, a: %{d: 2, c: 3}})
b = AshGraphLaw.Evidence.canonical_json(%{a: %{c: 3, d: 2}, b: 1})
{a, a == b}
```

## Tamper and detect

Change one bound fact, keep the old digest, and the recomputation disagrees. Evidence that
fails this check names no admission at all:

```elixir
tampered = %{evidence | input_digest: String.duplicate("f", 64)}

%{
  stored_digest_still_claims: tampered.digest == evidence.digest,
  recomputed_matches_stored?: AshGraphLaw.Evidence.digest(tampered) == tampered.digest,
  recomputed_differs_from_original?: AshGraphLaw.Evidence.digest(tampered) != evidence.digest
}
```

The engine identity is part of the digest too, so evidence cannot be re-attributed to another
engine build without changing the digest:

```elixir
other_engine = %{evidence | wasm_sha256: String.duplicate("1", 64)}
AshGraphLaw.Evidence.digest(other_engine) != evidence.digest
```

## Evidence data, as data

`to_map/1` yields a string-keyed map ready for JSON, which is how evidence leaves the process:

```elixir
map = AshGraphLaw.Evidence.to_map(evidence)
json = Jason.encode!(map)
decoded = Jason.decode!(json)
{Map.keys(decoded) |> Enum.sort(), decoded["digest"] == evidence.digest}
```

## Replay a real admission

Define a resource and admit the same input twice. The projection is deterministic, so the input
digest is identical across runs; the evidence digest is identical too when the engine answers
identically.

```elixir
defmodule Demo.Claim do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: Demo.Domain,
    data_layer: Ash.DataLayer.Ets,
    extensions: [AshGraphLaw.Resource]

  ets do
    private? true
  end

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key :id
    attribute :statement, :string, public?: true
  end

  actions do
    defaults [:read]

    create :assert do
      accept [:statement]
      change {AshGraphLaw.Change.Admit, admission: :claim_closure}
    end
  end

  graphlaw do
    admission :claim_closure do
      step(:rdfs)
    end
  end
end

defmodule Demo.Domain do
  use Ash.Domain, validate_config_inclusion?: false

  resources do
    resource Demo.Claim
  end
end
```

The input digest is decided before the engine: project the changeset and hash it. Two
changesets over the same input agree, and a different input disagrees:

```elixir
{:ok, admission} = AshGraphLaw.Admissions.fetch(Demo.Claim, :claim_closure)

project_digest = fn statement ->
  changeset = Ash.Changeset.for_create(Demo.Claim, :assert, %{statement: statement})
  {:ok, %{text: text}} = AshGraphLaw.Projection.Default.data(changeset, admission: admission)
  :crypto.hash(:sha256, text) |> Base.encode16(case: :lower)
end

%{
  same_input_same_digest?: project_digest.("water is wet") == project_digest.("water is wet"),
  different_input_different_digest?: project_digest.("water is wet") != project_digest.("fire is hot")
}
```

Now admit for real, twice, and compare whatever evidence the engine produced:

```elixir
case AshGraphLaw.Pool.start_link(size: 1) do
  {:ok, _pool} -> :started
  {:error, {:already_started, _pool}} -> :already_running
end

admit_once = fn ->
  parent = self()

  result =
    Demo.Claim
    |> Ash.Changeset.for_create(:assert, %{statement: "water is wet"})
    |> Ash.Changeset.after_action(fn changeset, record ->
      send(parent, {:evidence, changeset.context[:graphlaw]})
      {:ok, record}
    end)
    |> Ash.create()

  case result do
    {:ok, _record} ->
      receive do
        {:evidence, %AshGraphLaw.Evidence{} = ev} -> {:evidence, ev}
      after
        0 -> {:no_evidence}
      end

    {:error, error} ->
      {:refused, AshGraphLaw.Error.codes(error)}
  end
end

first = admit_once.()
second = admit_once.()

case {first, second} do
  {{:evidence, one}, {:evidence, two}} ->
    %{
      replayed?: true,
      input_digest_equal?: one.input_digest == two.input_digest,
      evidence_digest_equal?: one.digest == two.digest,
      recomputes?: AshGraphLaw.Evidence.digest(one) == one.digest,
      standing: one.standing
    }

  other ->
    %{replayed?: false, observed: other}
end
```

With the engine vendored, `replayed?` is `true` and both digest comparisons are `true`. Without
it, `replayed?` is `false` and `observed` shows the typed refusal for each attempt: there is no
evidence to replay, and the notebook says so instead of inventing any.

## What replay does and does not establish

Equal digests show the same input was observed by the same engine build and answered the same
way. They grant nothing: GraphLaw derives and validates, it never authorizes. Standing
`:PARTIAL_ALIVE` means the admission was observed; only an exact-SHA receipt raises a claim
further.
